("premiere",None,"Tristan und Isolde premiered in Munich on 10 June 1865, conducted by Hans von B\u00fclow.","premiered at the Königliches Hoftheater und Nationaltheater in Munich on 10 June 1865, with Hans von Bülow conducting"),
("blooper",None,"The premiere, planned for 15 May 1865, was postponed because the Isolde, Malvina Schnorr von Carolsfeld, was hoarse. Her husband Ludwig sang Tristan.",["The planned premiere on 15 May 1865 had to be postponed until the Isolde, Malvina Schnorr von Carolsfeld, had recovered from hoarseness","with Malvina's husband Ludwig partnering her as Tristan"],"Premiere"),
("history",None,"The opera was inspired partly by Schopenhauer\u2019s philosophy and by Wagner\u2019s relationship with his muse, Mathilde Wesendonck.","Tristan und Isolde was inspired in part by the philosophy of Arthur Schopenhauer, and by Wagner's relationship with his muse Mathilde Wesendonck."),
("composer",None,"Wagner to Liszt, 1854: \u201cNever in my life having enjoyed the true happiness of love I shall erect a memorial to this loveliest of all dreams.\u201d","Never in my life having enjoyed the true happiness of love I shall erect a memorial to this loveliest of all dreams","Composition_history","Wagner, letter to Franz Liszt (via Wikipedia)"),
("music",PRE,"The \u201cTristan chord\u201d in the Prelude marks a defining moment in music history: chromaticism, tonal ambiguity and prolonged harmonic suspension.",["Its advanced harmony, immediately announced by the Tristan chord of its prelude, marks a defining moment in the evolution of music","prolonged harmonic suspension"]),
("music",PRE,"The harmonic tension set up in the Prelude isn\u2019t fully resolved until the very end of Act III.","The cadences first introduced in the prelude are not resolved until the finale of act 3","Significance_in_the_development_of_Western_music"),
("critic",PRE,"Critic Eduard Hanslick, 1868: the Prelude reminded him of an old painting of a martyr whose intestines are slowly unwound on a reel.","reminds one of the old Italian painting of a martyr [Erasmus of Formia] whose intestines are slowly unwound from his body on a reel","Reactions","Eduard Hanslick (via Wikipedia)"),
("critic",PRE,"Emmanuel Chabrier reportedly wept at the opening: \u201cI\u2019ve been waiting for ten years of my life for that A on the \u2019cellos.\u201d","I've been waiting for ten years of my life for that A on the 'cellos","Reactions","Vincent d\u2019Indy, on Chabrier (via Wikipedia)"),
("critic",None,"Clara Schumann called Tristan \u201cthe most repugnant thing I have ever seen or heard in all my life\u201d.","the most repugnant thing I have ever seen or heard in all my life","Reactions","Clara Schumann (via Wikipedia)"),
("critic",None,"Verdi said, shortly before his death, that he \u201cstood in wonder and terror\u201d before Tristan.","stood in wonder and terror","Reactions","Giuseppe Verdi (via Wikipedia)"),
("artist",None,"Richard Strauss first said Wagner\u2019s music \u201cwould kill a cat\u201d. Later, after conducting his first Tristan: \u201cIt was the most wonderful day of my life.\u201d",["would kill a cat and would turn rocks into scrambled eggs","I have conducted my first Tristan.","It was the most wonderful day of my life."],"Reactions","Richard Strauss (via Wikipedia)"),
("critic",None,"Mark Twain at Bayreuth: \u201cI know of some, and have heard of many, who could not sleep after it, but cried the night away.\u201d","I know of some, and have heard of many, who could not sleep after it, but cried the night away.","Reactions","Mark Twain (via Wikipedia)"),
("music",DUET,"At the climax of the love duet, the expected resolution is cut off by Kurwenal\u2019s cry: \u201cRette Dich, Tristan!\u201d","where Tristan and Isolde gradually build up to a musical climax only to have the expected resolution interrupted by Kurwenal (\"Rette Dich, Tristan!\")","Significance_in_the_development_of_Western_music"),
("music",DUET,"Ravel parodied this love duet as a duet for two cats in L\u2019enfant et les sortil\u00e8ges.","Maurice Ravel parodied the Love Duet in the Duo miaulé in his opera L'enfant et les sortilèges","Reactions"),
("music",LIEB,"Only after Isolde\u2019s \u201cLiebestod\u201d does the music fully resolve; she sinks down, \u201cas if transfigured\u201d, onto Tristan\u2019s body.","Full resolution does not occur until the very end, after Isolde sings the passage commonly called the \"Liebestod\"","Significance_in_the_development_of_Western_music"),
("music",LIEB,"Wagner himself called the Prelude the \u201cLiebestod\u201d and Isolde\u2019s final aria the \u201cVerkl\u00e4rung\u201d (Transfiguration).","Wagner called the prelude the \"Liebestod\" (Love-death) while Isolde's final aria \"Mild und leise\" he called the \"Verklärung\" (Transfiguration).","Concert_extracts_and_arrangements"),
